Date: Sat, 13 Mar 93 22:20:58 CST
From: derek@cs.wisc.edu (Derek Zahn)
Subject: PHIL: Extropian magick?

Scott DeLancey continues the conversation on consciousness
and mystical phenomena:

>> So far, so good, so long as we don't
>> push that slippery term "consciousness" very far [but that's
>> a debate for another time].
> Actually that's the really interesting question.

Agreed; far more interesting than the other points, and even
Extropian in nature... after all, if we seek to extend or expand
consciousness, we should know what it is.  I refrain from the
attempt, however, because many many many very bright and rational
philosophers of mind have been splitting hairs on the issue for
so long that I have resigned myself to a rather pragmatic view.
I welcome attempts to define consciousness, though.

>From a strictly pragmatic view, some mystics even seem to have
a rather strong grasp on things, AS LONG AS they stick to the
phenomenalogical domain and avoid the mystical trappings.  For
example, in _How to Meditate:  A Practical Guide_ by Kathleen
McDonald (a Buddhist, and I will arbitrarily choose that book as
the source of examples because I actually sort of like it), the
mind is introduced:

    Mind changes from moment to moment.  It is a beginningless
    continuum, like an ever-flowing stream:  the pervious mind-
    moment gives rise to the next mind-moment, and so on.  It
    is the general name given to the totality of our conscious
    and unconscious experiences:  each of us is the centre of
    a world of thoughts, perceptions, feelings, memories, and
    dreams -- all of these are mind.

Fair enough!  Except for the flowery language, not too bad.
And the meditation exercises at least start out as being
quite interesting -- I can't see anything at all wrong
(read: non-extropian) with turning inward; exploring and
trying to get control (as you say, Scott) of the phenomenalogical
domain.  I have been fascinated at how hard it can be to
achieve "single-pointed concentration" and so on -- but
there's nothing mystical or irrational about that; it's
simple observation and self-transformation, which can actually
have some practical benefits re: concentration quite aside
from any aesthetic pleasure taken from self-discovery.

However, unfortunately, it soon changes -- first, to unfounded
(perhaps reasonable-sounding, to the credulous) metaphysical
dogma:

   Mental energy flows through the body within a psychic nervous
   system composed of thousands of thin, transparent, subtle
   channels.  The principal ones -- known as the central, right
   and left channels -- run parallel to and just in front of the
   spinal column.  Pure mental energy can function only within the
   central channel, whereas deluded energy flows through all the
   others.  At present, our central channel is blocked by knots
   of negative energy....

Of what use is this?  Both you and Eric seem to be saying
that stuff like this can be used as some sort of metaphorical
gateway to parts of the phenomenalogical domain, and that the
particular visualizations involved developed over long periods of
time to be more effective for doing just that.  Well, OK, but it
seems like a rather odd way to go about it, and once you change
to that vocabulary and belief structure, you've left your friends
in the dust.

And it gets worse.  The whole point of the exercises is actually
to put forward a mystical MORAL dogma -- whether it be the
interconnectedness of life, reverence for a world-spirit, or
whatever, I can't see why phenomenal investigations carefully
designed to support a particular (religious) set of values should
necessarily be optimal or even useful for those of us inclined
toward more rational transhumanist eupraxophies.

> I would be interested--though it may be of
> marginal relevance to  the list--in discussion of
> possible interpretations of mystical data on
> consciousness ...

It is of marginal relevance -- but if there are in fact
mystical data (virtually anonymous anecdotal accounts
are useless because we can come up with endless numbers
of contradictory ones regarding everything from astral
projection to UFO abductions to life stories of messiahs
in Waco, TX), by all means present some!  That's what I
asked you for regarding 'magick' -- which you included
in your original post as being relevant but are apparently
unable to produce any such data about.

> pulling your consciousness away from the word-stream and
> the stream of visual and auditory percepts that are your
> normal phenomenological environment.  OK, given a model
> of consciousness--Dennett's, say--can you make it make
> any predictions about what the effect of
> such an exercise might be?

Well, I can't speak for Dennett (we're discussing _Consciousness
Explained_, btw, which is a particular functionalist account
of mind that is compatible with rationality), but I would guess
that it might go something like this:

Many of the inner agencies involved in editing the multiple
drafts of conscious narrative are heavily based on language.
By reducing their effects, several things occur:  First, the
patterns of thought that would normally be in effect are suppressed,
which has the effect of allowing non-usual patterns to take over --
which could lead to various "creative" modes that have their uses
but you wouldn't want to wander around in traffic like that without
extensive retraining of the internal environment.  Second, 
as less attention is paid to internal linguistic activity (at
least that near "the surface"), more emphasis is placed on
other types of narrative (imagery, etc) and there should be
a heightening of tactile awareness -- but without conscious
linguistic classification.  Third, one's sense of time should
probably be skewed because it is often metered by the internal
dialogue.  Fourth, it should be darned difficult to maintain
such a state since the mind is trained for a different balance.
And so on.  Seem pretty clear?  (Apologies to Dennett if he lurks
here).

> If Wilson actually found any wheat in there, he must have been
> a magician himself to dig it out of the colorful but very fluffy
> chaff.  But I actually think he did. 

As a fan of _Illumanitus!_, I'm curious about this.  What book
of R.A. Wilson's are you talking about?

> Mystical techniques are all intended to teach you to take
> conscious control of your phenomenological environment.

Nothing wrong with that... nothing mystical about it either!!!

derek



