From: drw@BOURBAKI.MIT.EDU
Subject: ECON/LAW: Tragedy of the commons

Somebody (I forgot who) mentions that a good way to solve the "tragedy
of the commons" is to subdivide the commons by constructing a suitable
system of private ownership.

There still remains the problem of how to handle cases where private
ownership is difficult to arrange.  The ozone layer is a good example
of this, but vaccination and epidemic risk is a more subtle and
interesting one -- one's chances of getting a disease are strongly
dependent on the fraction of the populace that is vaccinated.  (And a
sizable fraction of those vaccinated are unavoidably not actually
protected.)

Another aspect of this that I don't understand is that some
libertarians seem to be opposed to intellectual property, that is,
they want to prevent the development of a system of private property
that would stimulate the production of useful information!

Dale

