> 
> What's fascinating about this is that it resembles the present legal
> climate -- within any single MUD, killing vs. non-killing is
> determined by an "authority"; the characters did not evolve an
> enforcement mechanism among themselves.  But the people/characters can
> "vote with their feet" between MUDs.  Similarly, if I want to live in
> a jurisdiction that allows me to smoke marijuana or carry a pistol, I
> can move to Montana or Jamaca.  So in a sense, the present mosaic of
> jurisdictions is about the same as the MUDs, except that the
> transaction costs of changing jurisdictions are higher than that for
> changing MUDs.

  Actually, this isn't the general case at all. On the majority of combat
muds (DikuMUDs) a system of outlawry exists.  If you get caught
stealing or killing another player, if becomes OK socially to kill you.
Most of the people on the mud will hunt you down and kill you to get your
gold/experience. On some muds, even the "good" guardian monsters 
(which will protect you if someone attacks you while they are watching)
will hunt down outlaws.  The only authority (software mechanism) that
prevents killing is one that prevents level-0/1 players from being killed
(so they won't be discouraged from playing).  However, some muds
have total anarchy and allow anything to be killed.

  Muds are a perfect example of spontaneous PPL and market economics.
Players go on quests, kill monsters, get rare equipment, and then
"auction" it for gold, which they use to buy other equipment that is
auctioned. The auction is not something sanctioned by the game, but emerged
spontaneously (now on most muds there is a separate channel for it though).
  Law concists of players forming "justice guilds" to hunt don't know
evil players, or players forming posses to go after thieves.


