From: szabo@techbook.com (Nick Szabo)
Subject: reason

Perry Metzger:
> When you deny reason and reality, you make it hard for yourself to
> function in the real world. If cause and effect cease to mean anything
> for you, what stops you from walking in front of cars? 

I laud Perry for recognizing that the consequences of a belief are 
as important as its _prima facia_ truth or falsity; perhaps moreso. 
But this conclusion quite overgeneralized; far too all-or-nothing.  Do
Mormons walk in front of cars more often because they believe that
spirits are governed from the planet Kolob?  I heavily doubt it.   
Nobody "denies reason and reality" in full; just inconvenient parts 
of it. The might also create convenient pieces of fiction, either 
helpful or harmful. 

Certainly there are voluminous examples where specific irrational beliefs 
have specific bad consequences, but in many other cases they may be harmless 
or even beneficial.  Whether or not this is the case should itself 
be the subject of rational inquiry; an _a priori_ judgement that 
irrationality is automatically harmful and never helpful is itself
irrational.

I suggest this extropian test for the value of a belief: what is the 
measurable entropic or anti-entropic effect of that belief, including
its indirect and long-term effects?  Since measuring this may not 
be easy, I'm looking for rule-of-thumb measures which reflect our
our goal of fighting entropy.

Motivations and values play important roles in determining the
consequences of a belief.  There exists a phenomenon I call 
_emergent rationality_ in which irrational beliefs can give rise to 
rational, motivated, extropic behaviors. Genetic evolution itself is 
an example: genes are in no way "rational"; they do not logically 
deduce what they should evolve into.  Instead apparent rationality 
and purposiveness emerge out irrational combinations and mutations 
of molecular strings and the selection action of the environment.

This phenomenon can be recreated on computers as well; a new method 
called genetic programming which Tim, Derek & I have mentioned 
can often design algorithms every bit as efficient as rational human
programmers.  As a simplified model of memetic/genetic interaction 
I suggest self-replicating robots, with the extropian benchmark 
being their rate of expansion through the galaxy. In this model 
significant genetic evolution (the robots' fixed ROM) does not occur 
during the timeframe of interest in memetic evolution (mutatable RAM). 
Which kinds of software facilitate or prevent such boundless expansion? 
Then look for analogious meme complexes in our current memetic/genetic 
environment.  Hopefully this abstract model will help us create
extropian memes with less influence by and rationalization of our
preconceived beliefs.

I heavily doubt we will find that all rational beliefs are more
anti-entropic than all irrational beliefs, merely based on their 
truth or falsity. My ideal is finding beliefs that are at the
same time true, motivational, and have extropian consequences.  I 
suspect this may be some "sexual recombination" of various religions, 
science, the current Extropian Principles, etc.  I suspect we have much
self-transformation to do before we find a sufficiently good
mix.  Let's find that mix based on rational judgement of the
consequences as well as the surface truth of many different kinds
of memes, instead of rejecting a huge population of often quite 
successful memes out of hand.

